"""SEN0401 chapter 8 - leaf concepts, part A: signature foundations, SIGHASH flags and schnorr signatures. A leaf is
(top, mid, leaf, example, definition, K-expression, teaching paragraph); the K-expression is executed by the checks and its value frozen in sen0401_ch08_expect_v1_0_0.py."""
__version__ = "1.0.0"
LEAVES_A = [
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1 foundations
 ("SignatureFoundations", "SignatureRoles", "VerifierInputs", "message, public key, signature",
  "A verifier needs exactly three inputs, the message, the public key and the signature, and the answer is yes only when all three belong together.",
  "K.verifier_inputs()",
  "A digital signature is checked by a verification function that takes three inputs: the message that was signed (in Bitcoin, a hash of a chosen part of the transaction), the public key of the signer, and the signature itself, and it answers yes or no. The chapter's script runs this with a test key and gets yes for the matching trio and no when the message is replaced by another, when the public key is replaced by another, or when the signature is replaced by one made with a different private key, which is the list [True, False, False, False]. This is why a node needs no secret and no connection to the signer to validate a payment: everything it needs is published in the transaction and in the output being spent (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SignatureFoundations", "SignatureRoles", "MessageIntegrity", "one flipped bit in the message",
  "A signature covers its message, so changing even one bit of the message makes the signature fail.",
  "K.tampered_message_fails()",
  "The third of the three purposes the book gives a digital signature is integrity: after the signer has signed, nobody can change the signed data without the change being noticed. The chapter's script signs a 32-byte digest, flips a single bit of it and verifies again, and the answer is False. In a Bitcoin transaction this means that once a spender has signed with the common flag SIGHASH_ALL, a miner or a relay node cannot redirect the payment to another address or change the amount, because the signature would no longer verify against the changed transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SignatureFoundations", "SignatureRoles", "KeyBinding", "a signature checked against a neighbour's key",
  "A signature verifies only against the public key that matches the private key that made it, which is what ties a spend to the owner of the coins.",
  "K.wrong_key_fails()",
  "The first purpose is authorization by the owner of the funds: the output being spent names a public key (or its hash), and only the holder of the matching private key can produce a signature that verifies against it. In the chapter's script a signature made with one private key is checked against the public key of the next private key along and the answer is False. The second purpose, which the book calls non-repudiation, follows because nobody else could have produced the signature and so the proof of authorization is undeniable; the scripts of chapter 7 are what ask the question 'does this signature verify for this key', and this chapter is what answers it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SignatureFoundations", "GroupArithmetic", "ScalarModulus", "the group order n against the field prime p",
  "The numbers in a signature equation (nonce, challenge, private key, response) are reduced modulo the order n of the curve's group, not modulo the field prime p that the book names.",
  "K.scalar_modulus()",
  "Modular arithmetic keeps numbers inside a fixed range by taking remainders, and Bitcoin uses two different moduli on the curve secp256k1: the prime p, which bounds the coordinates of points, and the order n, the number of points in the group generated by G, which bounds the scalars that multiply points. The book says that multiplications in the signature equations are performed modulo p; the executed check shows that this is not what a signature uses. With a test key, a nonce and a large challenge, the value s reduced modulo n makes the equation sG = kG + eX hold (True), the same value reduced modulo p makes it fail (False), and the two numbers are different (True), with p larger than n by more than 2 to the 128. The reason is that multiplying a point by n gives the point at infinity, so scalars repeat with period n and not with period p (Wuille et al., 2020) (SECG, 2010)."),
 ("SignatureFoundations", "GroupArithmetic", "SignRoundtrip", "sign, then verify",
  "Signing with a private key and verifying with the public key returns yes, the basic round trip every signature scheme must satisfy.",
  "K.roundtrip()",
  "The simplest test of any signature implementation is the round trip: sign a message with a private key, verify with the matching public key and expect yes. The chapter builds ECDSA and BIP340 schnorr from the curve arithmetic of chapter 4 in plain Python and starts every demonstration with this round trip, which gives True; the later tests then ask whether the library also agrees with independent software, namely the 19 official BIP340 test vectors, the 500 signature hash vectors of Bitcoin Core and the signatures that Bitcoin Core 31.1 makes on a private test network (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SignatureFoundations", "TransactionSigning", "InputIndependence", "two owners, two inputs, one transaction",
  "Each input of a transaction is signed separately, so owners of different inputs can sign the same transaction one after the other and neither signature can be moved to the other input.",
  "K.two_parties_two_inputs()",
  "A transaction can spend several coins at once, and each coin (input) carries its own signature made with the key that owns that coin. The book notes that inputs of different owners can be signed in a partially constructed transaction; the chapter's script builds a transaction with two inputs owned by two different keys, lets each owner sign only their own input with SIGHASH_ALL, and verifies both, then swaps the signatures and finds that both fail. The list [True, True, False, False] shows why: the message each owner signs is the transaction as seen from their own input, so a signature is bound to its input as well as to its key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SignatureFoundations", "TransactionSigning", "DigestCommitment", "changing an output changes the signed hash",
  "What a signature signs is a hash computed from selected fields of the transaction, so changing a committed field changes the hash and breaks the signature.",
  "K.sighash_digest_changes_with_output()",
  "A signature in Bitcoin is made not over the transaction bytes themselves but over a 32-byte digest computed from them by the signature hash algorithm, and the SIGHASH flag selects which fields go into the digest. The chapter's script computes the legacy digest of a transaction with two inputs and two outputs twice and gets the same value (True), then changes the amount of one output by one satoshi and gets a different value (False for equality), which is the whole mechanism of commitment: a field that is in the digest cannot change after signing, and a field that is not in the digest can (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)."),
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2 sighash
 ("SighashFlags", "FlagCoding", "FlagValues", "ALL = 1, NONE = 2, SINGLE = 3, ANYONECANPAY = 0x80",
  "The three base flags are the numbers 1, 2 and 3 and ANYONECANPAY adds 0x80, which gives the six combinations 1, 2, 3, 129, 130 and 131.",
  "K.flag_values()",
  "A SIGHASH flag is a single byte appended to a signature that says which parts of the transaction the signature covers. The base flags are ALL (1), NONE (2) and SINGLE (3), and the modifier ANYONECANPAY has the value 0x80, which is 128, so that it combines with each base flag by addition to give 129, 130 and 131, written 0x81, 0x82 and 0x83 in hexadecimal. The chapter's library lists them as [1, 2, 3, 128, 129, 130, 131], the values of the book's tables, and the next leaf shows that Bitcoin Core's source declares the same constants (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the partially signed transaction format that wallets pass between signers, each input can name the type its signature must use in the field PSBT_IN_SIGHASH_TYPE, key type 0x03 (Chow, 2017)."),
 ("SighashFlags", "FlagCoding", "CoreConstants", "interpreter.h of Bitcoin Core",
  "Bitcoin Core's header declares SIGHASH_ALL, NONE, SINGLE and ANYONECANPAY as 1, 2, 3 and 0x80 and adds SIGHASH_DEFAULT = 0 for taproot only.",
  "K.core_header_flags()",
  "The reference for what the network accepts is the code of Bitcoin Core, so the chapter reads the constants from the saved copy of src/script/interpreter.h at the pinned commit: SIGHASH_ALL = 1, SIGHASH_NONE = 2, SIGHASH_SINGLE = 3, SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY = 0x80, and a fifth constant SIGHASH_DEFAULT = 0 whose comment says it is for taproot only, is implied when the sighash byte is missing and is equivalent to SIGHASH_ALL. The list [1, 2, 3, 128, 0] is what the script reads, and the zero is the value the book's table does not have, because the book's table is about ECDSA signatures (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("SighashFlags", "FlagCoding", "TaprootHashTypes", "the seven valid values",
  "A taproot signature may use only the hash types 0x00, 0x01, 0x02, 0x03, 0x81, 0x82 and 0x83, and every other byte value makes the signature invalid.",
  "K.bip341_valid_hash_types()",
  "BIP341 defines the signature message of taproot and lists exactly the hash types that are allowed: the default 0x00 (which is written by leaving the byte out) and the six combinations 0x01, 0x02, 0x03, 0x81, 0x82 and 0x83. The chapter's implementation of the BIP341 signature message is run on all 256 byte values and returns a digest for exactly those seven, ['0x0', '0x1', '0x2', '0x3', '0x81', '0x82', '0x83'], and nothing for the other 249. Bitcoin Core enforces the same rule and the regtest run in a later leaf shows what it says about an explicit zero byte (Wuille et al., 2020)."),
 ("SighashFlags", "FlagCoding", "AnyprevoutValues", "0x40 and 0xc0 of BIP118",
  "The proposal BIP118 would add the flags SIGHASH_ANYPREVOUT = 0x40 and SIGHASH_ANYPREVOUTANYSCRIPT = 0xc0 to tapscript, and it is still only a draft.",
  "K.anyprevout_values()",
  "The book mentions BIP118 as a proposal that would let a signature leave out the identity of the coin being spent, so that one signature can be reused for any coin with the same script, which is the feature that the eltoo design for the Lightning Network needs. The chapter reads the values from the saved BIP text, 64 (0x40) for SIGHASH_ANYPREVOUT and 192 (0xc0) for SIGHASH_ANYPREVOUTANYSCRIPT, and the BIP index at the pinned commit still lists it with the status Draft, so it is not part of the rules that any node enforces and Bitcoin Core does not list it among the BIPs it implements (Decker and Towns, 2017)."),
 ("SighashFlags", "Commitments", "CommitmentMatrix", "which fields change the hash",
  "A matrix of five probes shows what each flag commits to: changing a field that the flag covers changes the hash of the signed input, changing one it does not cover leaves it unchanged.",
  "K.commitments_table()",
  "The chapter finds what each flag commits to by experiment: it takes a transaction with three inputs and three outputs, computes the hash that input 1 signs, changes one field at a time and records whether the hash changed. The probes are, in order, the outpoint of another input, the sequence number of another input, the output with the same index, the other outputs, and the input's own sequence number. The results are 0x01 ALL [1,1,1,1,1]; 0x02 NONE [1,0,0,0,1]; 0x03 SINGLE [1,0,1,0,1]; 0x81 [0,0,1,1,1]; 0x82 [0,0,0,0,1]; 0x83 [0,0,1,0,1]. The book's table says ALL, NONE and SINGLE sign 'all inputs', which holds for the outpoints but not for the sequence numbers of the other inputs, which NONE and SINGLE leave out, as BIP143 also says (Lau and Wuille, 2016)."),
 ("SighashFlags", "Commitments", "CoreSignatureSurvival", "does Core's own signature survive a change",
  "Signatures made by Bitcoin Core 31.1 with each flag survive exactly the changes the flag allows: NONE survives any change of outputs and SINGLE only a change of a different output.",
  "K.core_signature_survives_changes()",
  "The chapter asks Bitcoin Core 31.1, running on a private test network, to sign three-input segwit transactions with each flag, and then modifies a copy in two ways and verifies the first input's signature again with the chapter's own code: first the amount of the output with the same index as the input, then the amount of a different output. The answers, as [after the first change, after the second], are ALL [False, False], NONE [True, True], SINGLE [False, True], ALL|ANYONECANPAY [False, False], NONE|ANYONECANPAY [True, True] and SINGLE|ANYONECANPAY [False, True]. This confirms with independent software the claim of the book's table that SINGLE protects exactly the output at its own index, from one run of the node (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SighashFlags", "Commitments", "AnyoneCanPayEffect", "other inputs may change",
  "With ANYONECANPAY a signature stays valid when the other inputs are replaced, which is what lets strangers add inputs to a crowdfunding transaction.",
  "K.core_anyonecanpay_survives_other_input_change()",
  "The ANYONECANPAY modifier lets anybody add inputs to a transaction after one input has been signed, which the book uses for crowdfunding: the signer commits to the outputs that pay the goal and leaves the inputs open (and the Bitcoin Wiki's assurance contract example describes the same). The chapter changes the outpoint of the third input in each of the transactions that Bitcoin Core signed and checks the first input's signature again: it survives only with the ANYONECANPAY combinations, {'ALL': False, 'NONE': False, 'SINGLE': False, 'ALL|ANYONECANPAY': True, 'NONE|ANYONECANPAY': True, 'SINGLE|ANYONECANPAY': True}. The Lightning Network's BOLT 3 specification uses SIGHASH_SINGLE|SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY for the signatures of the second-stage transactions of channels with anchor outputs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Lightning BOLTs, 2026) (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)."),
 ("SighashFlags", "Commitments", "CoreFlagBytes", "the last byte of each signature",
  "The byte at the end of a signature Core made is the flag that was requested, and a taproot signature made with the default flag has 64 bytes and no flag byte.",
  "K.core_flag_bytes()",
  "To see the flag as the network sees it, the chapter reads the last byte of the signature in each transaction that Bitcoin Core signed: 0x1 for ALL, 0x2 for NONE, 0x3 for SINGLE, 0x81, 0x82 and 0x83 for the ANYONECANPAY combinations. For taproot (bech32m) outputs, the signature has 65 bytes with the same flag byte for the six explicit flags, and 64 bytes with no flag byte when the default is used, in line with the book's statement that a 64-byte signature uses the default hash and one more byte makes it 65 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SighashFlags", "Commitments", "CoreSignaturesVerify", "75 of 75 signatures",
  "Every one of the 75 signatures in the 25 transactions that Bitcoin Core 31.1 signed verifies with the chapter's own implementation of all four signature hash algorithms.",
  "K.core_flags_all_verify()",
  "As an independent check of the chapter's code, Bitcoin Core 31.1 signed 25 transactions of three inputs on a private test network: for each of the four address types legacy, p2sh-segwit, bech32 and bech32m, one transaction per flag (six), plus the taproot default. The chapter's library, which implements the legacy algorithm, the BIP143 algorithm for segwit version 0 and the BIP341 algorithm for taproot, recomputes the digest of every one of the 75 inputs and verifies the signature: the result is (75, 75). One run of the node on 15 October 2026 is the evidence; a different run would give different signatures but the same count (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("SighashFlags", "KnownPitfalls", "SingleBug", "a hash equal to the number one",
  "In the legacy algorithm, SIGHASH_SINGLE on an input that has no output of the same index does not sign a hash of the transaction but the number one.",
  "K.single_bug_equals_uint256_one()",
  "SIGHASH_SINGLE signs the output that has the same position as the input, but nothing in the original code handled an input whose position was larger than the number of outputs: the function returned the 256-bit number one, 0100...00, as if it were the hash, and every transaction with such an input then has the same digest. Bitcoin Core's source marks the line with a comment about the bug and returns uint256::ONE; the chapter reproduces it and finds the digest equal to the byte string '01' followed by 31 zero bytes (True). The book's table does not mention this case, and the Bitcoin Wiki's page on the signature hash procedure documents it (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)."),
 ("SighashFlags", "KnownPitfalls", "CoreRefusesSingleBug", "Bitcoin Core will not sign it",
  "Bitcoin Core 31.1 leaves such an input unsigned instead of creating the signature of the number one.",
  "K.core_refuses_single_bug_signature()",
  "Bitcoin Core's signing code explains why it should not sign such an input: the signature would commit to no output at all, so it would stay valid if the output were swapped, which the comment calls a footgun, and it ends 'So don't produce it.' The code returns without a signature, and the regtest run confirms it: asked to sign with SINGLE a two-input transaction with one output, the node signs the first input and reports for the second 'Unable to sign input, invalid stack size (possibly missing key)', so 'complete' is False, whereas the same transaction with two outputs is signed completely (True). The pair is [False, True] (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("SighashFlags", "KnownPitfalls", "SighashVectors", "Bitcoin Core's 500 vectors",
  "The chapter's legacy signature hash agrees with Bitcoin Core's own test file for all 500 vectors, which cover eight kinds of hash types.",
  "K.sighash_json_vectors()",
  "Bitcoin Core keeps a data file, src/test/data/sighash.json, with 500 random transactions, scripts, input indices and hash types together with the signature hash that the node must compute. The chapter's implementation of the legacy algorithm reproduces the digest in every one of the 500 vectors, and the vectors contain 8 different hash-type bytes (including values that are not valid flags, which the old algorithm treated as ALL), so the result is (500, 500, 8). This is the independent check that the single-purpose code in the chapter computes what the network computes (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3 schnorr
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "IdentityProtocol", "ToyProtocol", "x = 3, G = 5, k = 7, e = 11 with plain integers",
  "With ordinary integers the identification protocol works, s = k + e x satisfies s G = k G + e (x G), but anybody can divide by G, so the private key is exposed.",
  "K.toy()",
  "The book teaches the schnorr protocol first with whole numbers in place of curve points. Let the private key be x = 3 and the generator G = 5, so the public key is xG = 15; the nonce is k = 7, so the public nonce is kG = 35; the challenge is e = 11, and the response is s = k + e x = 40. The verifier checks that sG = 200 equals kG + e(xG) = 35 + 165 = 200, which holds. The chapter's script returns [15, 35, 40, 200, 165, True, 3] and the last number shows the weakness of the toy: dividing the public key 15 by G = 5 gives back the private key 3, which is exactly why the real protocol uses points on an elliptic curve, where this division is the discrete logarithm problem that nobody can solve (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "IdentityProtocol", "IdentityRound", "the three-move proof on secp256k1",
  "On the real curve the same three moves (commitment kG, challenge e, response s) are checked by one equation, sG = kG + eX, and it holds.",
  "K.identity_round_secp256k1()",
  "The interactive protocol has three moves: the prover sends a commitment, the public nonce kG; the verifier answers with a random challenge e; the prover replies with s = k + e x modulo n. The verifier then checks that s times G equals kG plus e times the public key X. The chapter runs one round on secp256k1 with a test private key, a test nonce and a challenge made from a hash, and the check returns True. The nonce is what hides the key: s alone is the key mixed with a random number, so it reveals nothing, but with a different challenge and the same nonce it would, which is why a nonce must be used once (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "IdentityProtocol", "WrongResponse", "s plus one",
  "If the response s is changed by even one unit, the verification equation no longer holds, so a prover without the private key cannot answer.",
  "K.identity_wrong_s()",
  "The soundness of the protocol is that only a prover who knows x can produce a response that satisfies the equation for a challenge that was chosen after the commitment. The chapter takes the correct response, adds one, and checks the equation again: the answer is False. A cheating prover who guesses a response must guess a 256-bit number, which has a probability of about 1 in 2 to the 256, and a prover who could answer two different challenges for one commitment could compute the private key from the two answers, which is what makes the proof convincing (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "IdentityProtocol", "ChallengeOrder", "told the challenge in advance",
  "A prover who learns the challenge before committing can pass without the private key, which is why the commitment must come first.",
  "K.impersonation_without_x()",
  "The order of the moves matters. If a cheater knows the challenge e in advance, they can pick any response s, compute the commitment as sG minus eX, and the check passes without the private key; the chapter does this with a test key and the check returns True. The book states the consequence: the nonce commitment must be fixed before the challenge is revealed, so in the signature version the challenge has to come from something the signer cannot choose after the fact, which is where the hash function enters (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "IdentityProtocol", "FiatShamirSignature", "a hash in place of the verifier",
  "The Fiat-Shamir transform replaces the verifier's random challenge by a hash of the commitment, the key and the message, which turns the interactive proof into a signature on that message.",
  "K.fiat_shamir_signature()",
  "A signature cannot wait for a verifier to answer, so the challenge is computed as e = hash(kG, xG, m), where m is the message: the signer commits to the nonce, derives the challenge from the hash, and responds. Fiat and Shamir described this transformation in 1986 and Schnorr applied it to his identification scheme. The chapter's simplified signature verifies for its message (True) and fails for a different message (False), the list [True, False]. Pointcheval and Stern (2000) give security arguments for digital signatures in the random oracle model, in which the hash function is treated as an ideal random object. Because the message is inside the hash, the signature is bound to it, and because the public key is inside the hash too, the signature is bound to the key, which is the key-prefixing property of the next leaf (Fiat and Shamir, 1986) (Schnorr, 1991)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Bip340Format", "Bip340Vectors", "19 official test vectors",
  "The chapter's BIP340 implementation matches all 19 vectors of the BIP, verifying 19 and reproducing the signature of each of the 8 vectors that give a secret key.",
  "K.bip340_vectors()",
  "BIP340 is the specification of the schnorr signature that Bitcoin uses in taproot, and its authors publish a table of test vectors with secret key, public key, auxiliary random data, message, signature and the expected verification result. The chapter's implementation gives the expected verification result for all 19 rows, including the invalid ones with wrong keys and out-of-range values, and for the 8 rows that include a secret key it also reproduces the public key and the exact signature. The result is [19, 19, 8, 8], which is the best evidence that the code follows the standard and not just the book's simplified protocol (Wuille et al., 2020)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Bip340Format", "SignatureSizes", "64 or 65 bytes",
  "A BIP340 signature has 64 bytes, the 32-byte x coordinate of the nonce point followed by the 32-byte scalar s, and in taproot one more byte may name a hash type.",
  "K.signature_sizes()",
  "The book explains the serialization: the signature is the x coordinate of kG (32 bytes) followed by the scalar s (32 bytes), 64 bytes in all and always the same length, unlike the variable length of ECDSA signatures. In a taproot input a 64-byte signature means the default hash type and a 65-byte signature carries an explicit hash type as its last byte. The chapter signs with the default and with an explicit flag and gets [(64, True), (65, True)], each size with a signature that verifies (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Wuille et al., 2020)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Bip340Format", "XOnlyKey", "32-byte keys with an even y",
  "A BIP340 public key is only the 32-byte x coordinate, and the signer negates the private key when the point's y coordinate is odd so that a key and its negative give the same public key.",
  "K.xonly_even_y()",
  "Bitcoin's compressed public keys have 33 bytes: a prefix for the parity of y and the 32-byte x. BIP340 saves the prefix by agreeing that the y coordinate is always the even one, so a public key is 32 bytes. If the real point has an odd y, the signer replaces the private key d by n minus d, whose point has the same x and an even y. The chapter shows for a test key that the x-only key has 32 bytes, that the parity of the point's y is 0 in this case, and that d and n minus d give the same x-only key, the list [32, 0, True]. The book simplifies this detail away when it says only the x coordinate of kG is included (Wuille et al., 2020)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Bip340Format", "TaggedHash", "SHA256(tag) twice as a prefix",
  "BIP340 hashes with a tag: the hash of the tag is computed once and put twice in front of the data, so that hashes made for different purposes cannot be confused.",
  "K.tagged_hash_prefix_len()",
  "The challenge e of BIP340 is not the plain SHA-256 of the data but a tagged hash, defined as SHA256(SHA256(tag) || SHA256(tag) || data) with the tag 'BIP0340/challenge'. The prefix is 64 bytes long, exactly one block of SHA-256, so an implementation can precompute the state after the prefix. Because every use of schnorr in Bitcoin has its own tag, a hash made for one purpose cannot be reused as a hash for another. The chapter's check confirms the length 64 for the prefix and that the tagged hash of a test input differs from the plain hash, the list [64, True] (Wuille et al., 2020)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Bip340Format", "DefaultNonce", "same input, same signature",
  "The default BIP340 signing function derives its nonce from the key, the message and auxiliary data, so with the same inputs it gives the same signature and with other auxiliary data another one.",
  "K.bip340_default_signing_deterministic()",
  "The book says BIP340 recommends a default algorithm for the nonce. It hashes the private key together with fresh auxiliary random data, hashes the result with the public key and the message to get k, and so the nonce depends on the message and the secret even if the auxiliary data is poor. The chapter signs the same message twice with the default (all-zero) auxiliary data and gets the same signature (True), signs once with other auxiliary data and gets a different one (True), and finds that the different signature also verifies (True): [True, True, True] (Wuille et al., 2020)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Bip340Format", "KeyPrefixing", "hash(R, P, m) against hash(R, m)",
  "Including the public key in the challenge stops an attacker who knows a signature for one key from deriving a signature for a related key such as a BIP32 child key.",
  "K.related_key_attack()",
  "The book explains that BIP340 commits to the public key in the challenge, hash(kG, xG, m), and the reason is practical. If the challenge were only hash(kG, m), then from a valid signature (kG, s) for the key P anybody could build, using public data only, a valid signature for the key P + aG, which is the key that unhardened BIP32 derivation would produce for the tweak a. The chapter implements both variants: without the key in the hash the forged signature verifies (True), with the key in the hash it fails (False), the list [True, False] (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Wuille, 2012)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Aggregation", "Linearity", "f(x + y + z) = f(x) + f(y) + f(z)",
  "Multiplying the generator by a scalar is additive and homogeneous, which is why signatures and keys of several signers can be added.",
  "K.linearity()",
  "A function is linear if it satisfies additivity, f(x + y) = f(x) + f(y), and homogeneity, f(a x) = a f(x). The map from a private key v to the point vG is linear, because it is repeated addition of G. The chapter checks both properties for chosen numbers, [True, True]. The schnorr response s = k + e x is also linear in the private key and the nonce, so the sums of the signers' keys, nonces and responses are the key, the nonce and the response of the combined signer, which is the property the next leaves use for multisignatures and batch verification (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Aggregation", "BatchVerification", "nine signatures, one equation",
  "Because the equation is linear, many signatures can be verified together as one random combination of their equations, and one bad signature in the batch makes the combined check fail.",
  "K.batch_equation()",
  "Verification of one schnorr signature checks sG = R + eP. Multiplying the equation of each signature by a random weight and adding them gives a single equation that is true if all signatures are valid and, with overwhelming probability, false if any is not; the weights stop a forger from arranging that two bad signatures cancel. The chapter takes the 9 valid signatures of the BIP340 test vectors, finds that the combined equation holds (True), swaps one signature for another and finds that it fails (False): [9, True, False]. The book's statement that batch verification takes about half the time of verifying one by one is not reproduced here, because the library's README lists no batch API (Wuille et al., 2020) (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Aggregation", "NaiveMultisig", "two keys added, two responses added",
  "Two signers can add their public keys and their signatures into one key and one signature that verifies, which looks like the signature of a single person.",
  "K.naive_multisig()",
  "In a scriptless multisignature the signers add their public keys into one aggregate key, add their nonces into one aggregate nonce, compute one challenge from the aggregates, and each returns a response; the sum of the responses is the signature. The chapter does this for two test signers and the combined signature verifies against the sum of their keys (True), and the sum is indeed the sum of the two public points (True): [True, True]. On the blockchain this is a single key and a single 64-byte signature, which is both smaller than a script multisignature and indistinguishable from a single-signer payment (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Aggregation", "KeyCancellation", "Bob publishes zG minus yG",
  "In the naive protocol the last signer to publish a key can choose it as their key minus the sum of the others, and then control the aggregate alone.",
  "K.key_cancellation()",
  "The naive protocol is unsafe. If Bob waits to see Alice's public key yG and then publishes the key zG minus yG, the aggregate is yG + (zG - yG) = zG, a key whose private key z only Bob knows, so Bob can sign alone for both. The book calls this the key cancellation attack and says that safe protocols must prevent it. The chapter performs it on the naive aggregation: the aggregate equals zG (True) and the signature Bob makes alone verifies against it (True): [True, True]. This is a rogue-key attack, and the MuSig family was designed against it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Maxwell et al., 2019)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Aggregation", "MuSigKeyAgg", "BIP327's four key aggregation vectors",
  "The MuSig2 key aggregation of BIP327 weights each key by a coefficient derived from all the keys, and the chapter's implementation reproduces the BIP's test vectors.",
  "K.musig2_keyagg_vectors()",
  "MuSig2 (Nick et al., 2021), standardized as BIP327 with the status Deployed, fixes the naive protocol by multiplying each signer's key by a coefficient that is a hash of the whole list of keys and that signer's own key, so that no one can choose a key as a function of the others' after seeing them. The chapter implements the BIP327 key aggregation algorithm and checks it against the BIP's own key aggregation test vectors, valid and invalid: the result is [4, 4], four checked and four matching. It is key aggregation only; the two-round signing is not implemented here (Nick et al., 2022) (Nick et al., 2021)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Aggregation", "MuSigBlocksCancellation", "the same attack against MuSig2 keys",
  "With BIP327's coefficients the aggregate of Alice's key and Bob's cancelling key is no longer Bob's key, so Bob cannot sign alone.",
  "K.musig2_blocks_cancellation()",
  "The chapter repeats the attack of the naive protocol against the aggregation of BIP327: Bob publishes zG minus yG after seeing Alice's key yG and the aggregate key is computed with the coefficients. The aggregate is not zG, so Bob's private key z does not control it, and the answer to the question 'is the aggregate Bob's key' is False. This is the property that the key cancellation attack breaks in the naive protocol and the reason Bitcoin Core can support the musig() descriptor for aggregated keys (Nick et al., 2022) (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "ThresholdSharing", "ShamirSharing", "a 2-of-3 polynomial",
  "Shamir's secret sharing hides a secret as the constant term of a polynomial and gives each holder one point, so that any two of three points rebuild the secret by interpolation.",
  "K.shamir_threshold()",
  "A threshold signature lets a subset of the key holders sign, for example any 2 of 3. The book builds it from the secret sharing of Shamir (1979): to share a secret d among three people so that any two can rebuild it, choose a random line f(x) = d + cx, give the person i the share f(i), and recover d from any two points by interpolating the line back to x = 0. In the chapter's scriptless protocol each of Alice, Bob and Carol shares their partial private key this way; Alice and Bob rebuild Carol's key from the two shares they hold, and together sign a message so that the signature verifies for the sum of the three keys. The three answers are [rebuilt key equals Carol's, signature verifies, aggregate key is the sum] = [True, True, True] (Shamir, 1979) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "ThresholdSharing", "AnyTwoOfThree", "all three pairs rebuild 31337",
  "Any two of the three shares give the same secret, here the number 31337.",
  "K.shamir_any_two_of_three()",
  "The chapter shares the number 31337 with a line, so that the three shares are three points of it, and rebuilds the secret from each of the three pairs of shares: the answer is 31337 each time, [31337, 31337, 31337]. Interpolation is done modulo n, the order of the curve, which is where the scalars of the signature live, and any k shares of a polynomial of degree k - 1 determine it uniquely, which is the theorem of Shamir's paper: knowledge of any k or more pieces makes the secret easily computable (Shamir, 1979)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "ThresholdSharing", "ShareSecrecy", "one share is consistent with every secret",
  "A single share of a 2-of-3 sharing reveals nothing, because for any other secret there is a line through the same point.",
  "K.single_share_hides_secret()",
  "The other half of the guarantee is secrecy: fewer than the threshold of shares give no information. The chapter takes one share (a point), picks a different secret, computes the slope that would make that secret's line pass through the same point, and finds that the share is the same, so the one point is consistent with every possible secret, and the answer is True. A holder of one share therefore learns nothing about the key, and a thief who steals one share cannot sign (Shamir, 1979) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "ThresholdSharing", "ManipulationAttack", "Bob tells each that the other is away",
  "In the book's simple threshold protocol a dishonest member can obtain the others' partial keys by claiming in turn that each of the others is unavailable.",
  "K.shamir_manipulation()",
  "The book warns that its scriptless threshold protocol has a flaw, which it calls manipulation: Bob tells Alice that Carol is unavailable, so Alice and Bob rebuild Carol's partial key from the shares they hold; then he tells Carol that Alice is unavailable, so Bob and Carol rebuild Alice's partial key. Bob now holds all three keys and can sign alone. The chapter performs it and gets [Carol's key rebuilt, Alice's key rebuilt, Bob holds the sum of all three] = [True, True, True]. The book says no scriptless threshold protocol had been proposed as a BIP; this is the kind of flaw that a standard protocol must remove (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
]
